Title: Richmond
Order: 4
Production Code: 1x04

FADE IN:

EXT. WINCHESTER - THE BLUE RIDGE - SUNSET

Belton Piedmont sits on a grassy knoll gazing at the Blue Ridge mountains as the light fades from the sky. The gloom of evening gathers; the mountains keep the same bold appearance. He has been four years at school; he has returned with his mother to Winchester. He has visited the old school house, its twisting walls and decaying floor.

Belton chooses the mountain as the emblem of his life and begs God to make him such in the moral world. He leaves Winchester to begin life in Richmond, the capital of the old Confederacy.

INT. RICHMOND COLORED SCHOOL - HALLWAY - MORNING

Through the influence of Mr. King, Belton has a position as a teacher in one of the colored schools. The principal is white; all the other teachers are young colored women. The principal carries him around to introduce him.

PRINCIPAL
Miss Nermal, allow me to present to you our newly arrived associate in the work, Mr. Belton Piedmont.

INT. RICHMOND COLORED SCHOOL - MISS NERMAL'S ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Antoinette Nermal has her back to them, gazing out of the window. Belton is so impressed with the loveliness of her form that he is sorry when she turns. Turned full round: a light brown skin, black eyes, thin well-formed lips. Her own lips come slightly apart.

ANTOINETTE NERMAL
Mr. Piedmont, we are glad to have a man of your acknowledged talents in our midst and we anticipate much of you.

BELTON
Thank you.

ANTOINETTE NERMAL
I trust that you will find our association agreeable.

BELTON
Oh assuredly, Miss Nermal. I am already more than satisfied that I shall expect much joy and pleasure from my association with you--I--I--I mean the teachers.

He looks uneasily at the principal and follows him on to the next room.

INT. RICHMOND COLORED SCHOOL - TEACHERS' NOON TABLE - DAY

The young lady teachers discuss the newcomer. Those he met before Miss Nermal's room think him "very nice." Those he met after think him rather dull.

ANTOINETTE NERMAL
Just grand.

The girls look at her inquiringly; she is understood to pass young men by unnoticed.

EXT. RICHMOND - STREET - AFTERNOON

A group of teachers walking home. One by one they drop off at their gates. Belton has contrived to get by Miss Nermal's side. When the rest are gone, she has some distance yet to go. Belton is a happy fellow.

SUPER: "FIVE EVENINGS IN EACH WEEK."

He escorts her from the school-house door to her own front yard. He has the privilege of calling at her residence and confines his visits to her house.

They do not talk of love. Any one who saw them together could tell at a glance what is in each heart.

INT. A PARTY - NIGHT

A social gathering of young people. Belton is Miss Nermal's escort. Pinning on the donkey's tail, going to Jerusalem, menagerie. Miss Nermal has so vigorously opposed kissing games that they have long since been dismissed from the best circles.

Belton has posted two or three young men to suggest "In the well." They do, just for the fun of an old time game.

Chairs pushed to the walls. A young man in the middle of the floor.

PLAYER
I am in the well.

QUESTIONER
How many feet?

PLAYER
Three feet.

QUESTIONER
Whom will you have to take you out?

Whosoever is named must go to him and kiss him as many times as he has feet. Miss Nermal's views are well known; the young men all pass her by. A young lady calls Belton to the well to kiss her out. He is so excited he forgets to kiss her. It pleases Antoinette immensely.

BELTON
I am in the well.

QUESTIONER
How many feet?

BELTON
Only one.

QUESTIONER
Whom will you have to take you out?

Belton is dazed, astounded at his own temerity. He averts his head.

BELTON
Miss Antoinette Nermal.

All eyes are directed to Miss Nermal to see her refuse. She casts a look of defiance around the room and calmly walks to where Belton stands. Their eyes meet. They understand each other. Belton presses those sweet lips and sits down the happiest of mortals.

Miss Nermal is left in the well. Belton glares around the room furiously. Who will be base enough to dare kiss that angel?

ANTOINETTE NERMAL
The person who put me in here will have to take me out.

Belton bounds from his seat. Miss Nermal's act breaks up that game.

EXT. MISS NERMAL'S GATE - NIGHT

They walk home. Neither speaks. At her gate she opens it, enters, and stands on the other side facing him. He stoops and in silence presses a lingering kiss on her lips. She does not move.

BELTON
I am in the well.

ANTOINETTE NERMAL
(whispering)
I am too.

BELTON
I shall always be in the well.

ANTOINETTE NERMAL
So shall I.

He plucks the gate open and clasps her to his bosom. He leads her to a double seat in the middle of the lawn and there, with the pure-eyed stars gazing down on them, they pour out their love.

Two hours later he leaves her and, at that late hour, rouses every intimate friend he has in the city to tell them of his good fortune.

INT. RICHMOND - PRINTING OFFICE - DAY

Belton wants to marry; his salary is fifty dollars a month. The colored people of Richmond have no newspaper or printing office. He has organized a joint stock company, started a weekly journal, and a job printing establishment. The paper takes well.

It begins to lift up its voice against frauds at the polls and champions honest elections. One article is copied in *The Temps* and commented on editorially.

INT. RICHMOND - POLITICIANS' ROOM - DAY

A search for the author. It is traced to Belton. The politicians give the school board orders to dump him forthwith: they cannot afford to feed and clothe a man who would so vigorously "attack Southern Institutions," meaning the universal practice of thievery and fraud at the ballot box.

Belton is summarily dismissed. The other teachers are warned to give no further support to his paper on pain of losing their positions. He is forced to give up the enterprise. His marriage is indefinitely postponed.

INT. RICHMOND - CAMPAIGN - DAY

Belton espouses the cause of the Republicans in a congressional and presidential campaign and does yeoman service. Owing to frauds, the Democrats carry the district, but the vote is closer than ever. The Republicans carry the nation and the President appoints a white republican postmaster of Richmond.

INT. RICHMOND POST OFFICE - DAY

SUPER: "STAMPING CLERK."

Belton, who by ability and service is entitled to the best place in the district in the gift of the government, is appointed stamping clerk at sixty dollars per month. The color of his skin is against him.

INT. RICHMOND - CHURCH - DAY

At the expiration of one year Belton proudly leads the charming Antoinette Nermal to the marriage altar. The most notable social event ever known among the colored people of Richmond. All the colored people and many of the white people of prominence at the reception; costly presents pour in.

INT. RICHMOND POST OFFICE - DAY (A YEAR LATER)

About two years from his appointment and one year from his marriage, a congressional convention is held. Belton's chief, the postmaster, wants a personal friend to have the nomination. The man is known to be prejudiced against colored people. Belton supports another candidate and wins for him the nomination.

The postmaster dismisses him from his position as clerk.

INT. PIEDMONT HOME - RICHMOND - NIGHT

Crushed in spirit, Belton comes home to tell his wife. She cheers him, bidding him not be despondent: a man of his talents would beyond all question be sure to succeed in life.

INT. RICHMOND - STREETS - DAY

He casts around for another occupation and sees no hope. He has a first class college education and nothing else. White people will not employ him and colored people have no enterprises in which they can employ him. Street laborer, hod-carrier, cart driver, factory hand, railroad hand are open to him, but society forbids a man of education that labor.

He finds scores of young men just in his predicament, gathered in groups and talking. They grow to hate a flag that floats undisturbed over such a condition of affairs. They wish for some foreign power to come in and bury it in the dirt.

Belton asks himself whither the nation is drifting. He might have joined this army of malcontents but a remarkable and novel idea occurs to him: to find out what view the white people are taking of the Negro.

INT. RICHMOND - HOME - DAY

He leaves Richmond, giving the people to understand that he has gone to get a place to labor to support his wife. Speculation is rife; many think he has deserted her. He arranges to visit her twice a month.

INT. NEW YORK - WIG-MAKER'S SHOP - DAY

He buys a wig representing the hair on the head of a colored woman, made especially to his order, and an outfit of well fitting dresses and other garments worn by women. The Nurse's Disguise.

INT. PIEDMONT HOME - RICHMOND - DAY

He reappears in Richmond clad as a woman. His wife and most intimate friends fail to recognize him. He reveals his identity to his wife and no one else.

INT. A LEADING WHITE MAN'S HOUSE - RICHMOND - DAY

A healthy, handsome, robust colored girl, with features rather large for a woman but attractive. Belton, as a nurse, loiters near the family circle. His ear is at keyholes. Sometimes he engages them in conversation on the question of the Negro.

He finds the white man utterly ignorant of the Negro of to-day and not bothering his brain about him. The barbers, the nurses, cooks and washerwomen, the police column of the newspapers, comic stories and minstrels are the sources of the white man's conception of the Negro.

INT. A LEADING WHITE MAN'S HOUSE - STAIRS - NIGHT

The young men have a poor opinion of the virtue of colored women. Time and again they try to kiss Belton, and he must exert his full strength to keep them at a distance. At every chance he talks to those who approach him of virtue and integrity. He soon has the name of a "virtuous prude."

Midnight carriage rides are offered and refused. Trips to distant cities proposed and declined. Money offered freely and lavishly. They begin to doubt his sex. A number of them resort to kidnapping and overpowering Belton.

After that eventful night Belton does no more nursing. They do not recognize who he is. He secretly leaves, has it announced that Belton Piedmont will return to Richmond in a short time, throws off his disguise, and appears as Belton Piedmont of old. The town is agog over the male nurse. None suspects him.

INT. PIEDMONT HOME - RICHMOND - NIGHT

Antoinette bears her deprivations like a heroine, smiling and cheerful. Not one sigh, not one word of complaint. She teaches Belton to hope and have faith in himself.

And now the Lord is about to bless him with an offspring. Belton hardly knows whether to be thankful or sorrowful.

INT. PIEDMONT HOME - ANTOINETTE'S ROOM - EVENING

As the time draws near Belton grows more moody and despondent. One evening the nurse calls him into his wife's room, bidding him come and look at his son. The nurse stands in the door and looks hard at Belton. He lifts the lamp from the dresser and approaches. Antoinette turns toward the wall and hides her head under the cover.

Eagerly, tremblingly, Belton pulls the cover from the little child's face.

INSERT - THE INFANT

The Piedmont Child. The color of Antoinette is brown. The color of Belton is dark. The child is white.

A terrible shriek breaks from Belton's lips. He drops the lamp on the floor and flees out of the house and rushes madly through the city.

EXT. THE ROAD TO WINCHESTER - NIGHT

Months and months. Sadly, disconsolately, broken in spirit, he drags himself to his mother's cottage at Winchester.

INT. PIEDMONT COTTAGE - WINCHESTER - NIGHT

His brothers and sisters have married and left the maternal roof. Belton sleeps in the loft from which in childhood he tumbled down when disturbed about the disappearing biscuits. His feelings toward his wife are more of pity than reproach. Like the multitude, he supposes his failure to support her tempted her to ruin. He loves her still, more passionately than ever.

Tenderly he lays the image of Antoinette to rest in a grave in the very center of his heart. Belton has lost all hope of personal happiness in this life. He is better prepared than ever to give his life wholly to the righting of the wrongs of his people.

INT. RICHMOND - CHURCH - DAY (THE SAME MONTHS)

Antoinette Nermal Piedmont has been tried and excluded from her church on the charge of adultery. She does not appear at the trial nor speak a word in her own defense. Society drops her as you would a poisonous viper.

EXT. RICHMOND - STREET - DAY

She walks, ostracised, undisturbed, her child on her arm. The only person about whom she is concerned is Belton.

CUT TO:
